  "category": "Entity-Relationship",
  "scenario": "A core banking ledger schema has two modeling bugs: LedgerLine is wired directly to Customer instead of Account, and Transfer is mistakenly wired directly to Customer instead of anchoring to JournalEntry. Meanwhile, Account -> LedgerLine and JournalEntry -> LedgerLine are missing.",
  "goal": "Repair the ledger so Customers own Accounts, LedgerLines link Accounts to JournalEntries, and each Transfer maps 1:1 to a posted JournalEntry.",
  "learningObjective": "Enforce double-entry accounting integrity across Customer, Account, LedgerLine, JournalEntry, and Transfer.",
  "hints": [
    "Find the two invalid edges attached to Customer that bypass Account or JournalEntry.",
    "Notice Transfer has a UNIQUE entry_id referencing JournalEntry (1:1), while LedgerLine links Account and JournalEntry (1:N).",
    "Delete Customer -> LedgerLine and Customer -> Transfer; keep Customer -> Account and connect Account -> LedgerLine, JournalEntry -> LedgerLine, and JournalEntry -> Transfer (1:1)."
  ],
  "solutionExplanation": "Customers own Accounts (1:N), every LedgerLine belongs to both an Account and a JournalEntry (1:N), and each Transfer posts via a single unique JournalEntry (1:1).",
